r=add(24,'Zanahorias Tiernas a la Mayordoma',4,40,'Quartered young carrots cook in an herb-scented butter sauce finished with a gently cooked yolk liaison.',[carrot(500),butter(25),flour(8),parsley(5),stock(150),salt(2),pepper(),yolks(2),water(30)],['Quarter {{carrot}} lengthwise, cutting very thick quarters into thinner batons. Melt {{butter}} in a wide saucepan, add the carrots and {{parsley}} and cook gently for 3 minutes without browning.','Sprinkle in {{flour}} and stir for 1 minute. Gradually add {{stock}}, {{salt}} and {{pepper}}. Cover and simmer for 15–25 minutes until tender, stirring occasionally; add a splash of water if needed.','Whisk {{yolks}} with {{water}}. Gradually whisk in a little hot sauce, then return to the pan. Stir gently over low heat until the sauce reaches 71°C (160°F), without boiling. Spoon over the carrots and serve.'],'Two yolks are selected from the source two-or-three range. Measured stock and fat amounts replace unspecified spoonfuls. Controlled cooking of the yolk liaison replaces pouring uncooked yolk onto the finished dish.')
r=add(28,'Zanahorias en Mantequilla with Mushroom Velouté',4,55,'Blanched carrots reduce in buttery broth, then receive herbs, a little pale mushroom sauce and crisp fried bread.',[carrot(500),water(1000),salt(3),butter(30),stock(250),sugar(3),parsley(5),I('sauceportion',15,'ml','prepared mushroom sauce reserved from the component in these steps','Use the sauce made below; this is a measured portion of that output, not an additional purchase.'),bread(60),oil(15)]+veloute(),veloutesteps()+['Measure {{sauceportion}} from the finished sauce for the carrots. Cool and refrigerate the remaining sauce promptly for another use.','Cut {{carrot}} into 3 mm rounds. Bring {{water}} and {{salt}} to a boil, blanch the carrots for 5 minutes and drain.','Put the carrots in a wide saucepan with {{butter|0.6666666666666666}}, {{stock}} and {{sugar}}. Simmer for 10–15 minutes until nearly tender, then uncover and reduce the liquid until it just coats them.','Stir in {{butter|0.3333333333333334}}, {{parsley}} and the measured prepared sauce. Heat gently for 1 minute.','Cut {{bread}} into cubes. Heat {{oil}} in a skillet and fry the cubes for 4–6 minutes, turning, until golden and crisp. Serve with the carrots.'],'Salsa rizada is identified from the same-book sauce entries as a pale stock-and-mushroom roux sauce, not a literal curled garnish. A complete small sauce batch using prepared stock is supplied; only 15 ml is used here and the rest is reserved. The translated quarter-liter is a modern 250 ml choice, not verified conversion of cuartillo. Butter amounts and croutons are explicit.')
r['source']+=' Component source retained: Salsa Rizada, source ID historical-48974af04dedab0a796de725, same book. The prepared-stock component is a disclosed shortcut to its long meat-stock preparation.'
r=add(32,'Sustancia de Zanahorias (Slow Carrot Purée Sauce)',8,230,'Carrots and onions simmer slowly in butter and stock, then are sieved and finished with a little pale mushroom sauce.',[carrot(1000),onion(250),butter(76),stock(1000),sugar(8),I('sauceportion',20,'ml','prepared mushroom sauce reserved from the component in these steps','Use the measured sauce made below, not an additional ingredient purchase.')]+veloute(),['Slice {{carrot}} lengthwise and quarter {{onion}}. Melt {{butter}} in a heavy saucepan and cook the vegetables gently for 5–8 minutes, stirring, without dark browning.','Add {{stock}} and {{sugar}}. Cover loosely and simmer over very low heat for 3 hours until the carrots fall apart when pressed with a spoon. Add small splashes of water to prevent drying.']+veloutesteps()+['Measure {{sauceportion}} from the prepared mushroom sauce. Cool and refrigerate the unused sauce promptly.','Lift the carrot and onion into a sieve, reserving their cooking broth. Mash and press through the sieve, using the reserved broth a little at a time to help the purée pass through.','Return the purée and all remaining carrot broth to the pan with the measured mushroom sauce. Simmer gently for 5–15 minutes, stirring and skimming excess surface fat, until thick enough to coat a spoon. Serve as a sauce alongside cooked meat or vegetables.'],'A roughly one-third source batch uses 1 kg carrots, 76 g butter and 20 ml finishing sauce; onion and stock weights are modern choices. The full three-hour simmer and sieving method remain. Salsa rizada is expanded as a small prepared-stock mushroom sauce component, a disclosed shortcut to the long source stock. No raw concentrated stock or missing sauce reference remains.','Sauces')
r['source']+=' Component source retained: Salsa Rizada, source ID historical-48974af04dedab0a796de725, same book.'
r=add(33,'Zanahorias con Azúcar in Light Roux Sauce',4,40,'Boiled carrots warm in a lightly sweetened butter-and-stock sauce.',[carrot(500),water(1000),salt(3),butter(25),flour(16),stock(300),sugar(8)],[boil(),'Melt {{butter}} in a saucepan, stir in {{flour}} and cook for 2 minutes. Gradually whisk in {{stock}} and simmer for 5 minutes until smooth.','Add the drained carrots and {{sugar}}, stir gently and simmer for 2–3 minutes until hot.'],'Two sugar lumps become a disclosed 8 g. Carrot, butter and stock quantities are supplied, and the source roux steps are expanded.')
r=add(34,'Zanahorias en Verde (Carrots in Parsley-Lemon Sauce)',4,40,'Tender carrots are coated in a green parsley sauce brightened with lemon juice.',[carrot(500),water(1000),salt(3),butter(25),flour(16),parsley(15),stock(300),pepper(),lemon(15)],[boil(),'Melt {{butter}} in a saucepan, add {{flour}} and {{parsley|0.3333333333333333}} and cook gently for 2 minutes. Gradually whisk in {{stock}}, add {{pepper}} and simmer for 5 minutes.','Finely mince {{parsley|0.6666666666666667}} and stir into the sauce with {{lemon}}. Add the carrots and warm gently for 2 minutes before serving.'],'The incomplete white-sauce and parsley-sauce references are replaced by one complete modern parsley roux sauce. Missing butter and liquid quantities are supplied. Parsley is split between cooking and a fresh green finish; this is a disclosed practical reconstruction, not a recovered exact sauce formula.')
r=add(37,'Cenouras Guisadas with White Wine',4,40,'Carrot slices brown lightly in fat and sugar before finishing in a mixture of broth and white wine.',[carrot(500),lard(25),salt(2),pepper(),sugar(13),stock(240),I('wine',240,'ml','dry white wine','Use 220 ml unsalted vegetable stock plus 20 ml lemon juice for an alcohol-free variation.')],['Slice {{carrot}} into 5 mm rounds. Heat {{lard}} in a wide saucepan, add the carrots, {{salt}}, {{pepper}} and {{sugar}}, and cook for 5–8 minutes, turning often, until lightly golden.','Take the pan briefly off the heat and carefully add {{stock}} and {{wine}}. Return to a gentle simmer and cook for 15–25 minutes until tender.','Uncover for the last few minutes to reduce the liquid to a light coating. Serve warm.'],'The source cup of broth and cup of wine become 240 ml each. Missing carrot and fat weights are supplied. This is a direct sauté-and-braise, unlike the boiled carrot and wine-roux version. Wine remains alcoholic after cooking.')
r=add(38,'Cenouras Guisadas with Lemon and Egg Yolk',4,45,'Carrots braise with onion and parsley in a sweet-tart lemon sauce finished with a gently cooked yolk.',[flour(8),onion(100),parsley(5),lard(28),carrot(500),sugar(13),salt(2),water(480),lemon(30),yolks(1)],['Melt {{lard}} in a saucepan and soften finely chopped {{onion}} and {{parsley}} for 5 minutes. Sprinkle in {{flour}} and stir for 2 minutes.','Gradually add {{water}}. Add {{carrot}} cut in 5 mm slices, {{sugar}}, {{salt}} and {{lemon}}. Cover loosely and simmer for 20–25 minutes until the carrots are tender.','Whisk {{yolks}} in a bowl, slowly whisk in a little hot sauce, then return to the saucepan. Stir gently over low heat until the sauce reaches 71°C (160°F), without boiling. Serve hot.'],'Two source cups of water become 480 ml. The undefined glass of lemon juice is deliberately set at 30 ml for a balanced modern batch, not asserted as a historic glass conversion. Onion is softened before flour is added, and egg heating is controlled.')
r=add(39,'Cenouras Guisadas com Hervilhas',6,40,'Young carrots and peas cook in a light wine sauce scented with parsley and fresh coriander.',[flour(4),butter(28),water(480),I('wine',120,'ml','dry white wine','Use 110 ml unsalted vegetable stock plus 10 ml lemon juice for an alcohol-free variation.'),salt(3),parsley(5),I('cilantro',5,'g','fresh cilantro leaves, chopped','Use the same weight of parsley for a different flavor.'),carrot(500),I('peas',300,'g','green peas, fresh or frozen','Use the same weight of frozen peas, adding directly near the end.')],['Melt {{butter}} in a saucepan, stir in {{flour}} and cook gently for 2 minutes. Gradually whisk in {{water}} and {{wine}}, then add {{salt}}, {{parsley}} and {{cilantro}}.','Cut {{carrot}} into 5 mm rounds. Add to the simmering sauce, cover loosely and cook for 15–20 minutes until nearly tender.','Add {{peas}} and simmer for 5–10 minutes until both vegetables are tender. Serve with the light cooking sauce.'],'The source water cups become 480 ml, the wine glass 120 ml, and missing vegetable weights are supplied. Fresh coriander is selected as the herb interpretation. Peas are added later than carrots for even tenderness; wine remains alcoholic after cooking.')
r=add(91,'Carrots à la Maître d’Hôtel with Lemon-Yolk Sauce',6,65,'Carrots cooked in bouillon are warmed with herbs and served in a light lemon, parsley and egg-yolk sauce.',[carrot(600),dict(stock(1000),key='cookstock'),butter(14),I('thyme',2,'g','fresh thyme leaves','Use 0.25 tsp dried thyme.'),parsley(5),bay(),salt(2),pepper()]+roux('s',14,8,480,'unsalted prepared beef consommé')+[lemon(15),I('sparsley',5,'g','fresh parsley, chopped for the sauce','Use the same weight of chives.'),yolks(1)],['Put {{carrot}} whole in a saucepan with {{cookstock}}. Simmer for 25–40 minutes until tender. Lift out the carrots; cool and refrigerate the cooking stock promptly for another use.','Cut the carrots into bite-sized pieces. Melt {{butter}} in a skillet, add the carrots, {{thyme}}, {{parsley}}, {{bay}}, {{salt}} and {{pepper}}, and warm gently for 10 minutes, turning. Add a little water if needed to prevent catching. Remove the bay leaf.']+rouxsteps('s')+['Add {{lemon}} and {{sparsley}} to the sauce and simmer gently for a further 10 minutes.','Whisk {{yolks}} in a bowl. Gradually whisk in a little hot sauce, then return to the pan and stir gently until the sauce reaches 71°C (160°F), without boiling. Pour over the hot carrots and serve.'],'The exact same-book Maître d’Hôtel sauce was recovered: it uses consommé, flour, lemon, parsley and egg yolk, rather than only herb butter. All components and controlled egg heating are included. Carrot and herb quantities are modern estimates.')
r['source']+=' Component source retained: Maître d’Hotel Sauce, same book, source ID historical-6867593ce9e1aed99ac5116d.'
r=add(145,'Gelbe Rüben with Butter Sauce',6,45,'Carrot pieces simmer with butter and sugar, then finish in a light stock-based butter sauce with parsley.',[carrot(750),butter(80),sugar(13),water(350),parsley(2)]+roux('s',20,20,250,'unsalted prepared light beef stock'),['Cut {{carrot}} into even 1 cm pieces. Put in a saucepan with {{butter}}, {{sugar}} and {{water}}. Cover and simmer for 20–30 minutes until tender, shaking gently instead of stirring vigorously. Add a little water if needed to prevent drying.']+rouxsteps('s')+['Uncover the carrots and let excess cooking water evaporate for a few minutes. Fold in all the prepared butter sauce and {{parsley}}, simmer gently for 2 minutes and serve.'],'The missing kohlrabi quantity reference becomes a modern 750 g carrot batch. Butter is deliberately set at 80 g rather than treating the extracted eight ounces as a verified original measure. A full 250 ml stock sauce replaces the butter-sauce reference; its roux amounts are modern choices for a pourable finish.')
r['source']+=' Component source retained: Butter-Sauce, same book, source ID historical-8fa5f716efd63b7ecddd5c83; its uncertain extracted proportions are not claimed as verified.'
r=add(151,'Carrots in Poulette Sauce',6,50,'Tender carrot pieces are coated in a rich chicken-stock and cream sauce finished with lemon and cooked egg yolks.',[carrot(750),water(1000,'soakwater'),water(1000),salt(3),butter(7)]+roux('s',42,24,240)+[cream(120),yolks(2),lemon(7.5)],['Cut {{carrot}} into 1 cm cubes or strips. Bring {{soakwater}} to a boil, pour over the carrots in a heatproof bowl and let stand for 5 minutes. Drain.','Bring {{water}}, {{salt}} and {{butter}} to a boil in a saucepan. Add the carrots and simmer for 10–15 minutes until tender, then drain.']+rouxsteps('s')+['Stir {{cream}} into the sauce and heat until steaming. Whisk {{yolks}} in a bowl and gradually whisk in a few spoonfuls of the hot sauce.','Return the egg mixture to the pan and stir gently over low heat until it reaches 71°C (160°F), without boiling. Stir in {{lemon}}, fold in the drained hot carrots and serve.'],'The referenced Macédoine à la Poulette sauce was read in the primary book transcription: butter, flour, chicken stock, cream, yolks and lemon. It is fully expanded here with modern gram/ml quantities and explicit egg cooking. The separate initial hot-water standing step is retained.')
r['source']+=' Verified component: https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Boston_Cooking-School_Cook_Book/Chapter_19#Macedoine_of_Vegetables_à_la_Poulette'
r=add(153,'Stewed Carrots and Onion in Drawn Butter',4,55,'Part-boiled carrots finish slowly with minced onion and parsley in a thin drawn-butter sauce.',[carrot(500),water(1000),salt(3),onion(160),parsley(5),pepper(),I('swater',90,'ml','drinking water for the drawn butter','Use the same volume of whole milk for a richer sauce.'),I('sflour',2.5,'g','all-purpose flour','Use the same weight of a gluten-free flour blend suitable for sauces.'),I('sbutter',28,'g','unsalted butter for the sauce','Use the same weight of a block-style dairy-free butter alternative.'),water(60,'braisewater')],['Cut {{carrot}} into 1 cm cubes. Bring {{water}} and {{salt}} to a boil, cook the carrots for 8–10 minutes until partly tender, and drain.','Whisk {{sflour}} with {{swater}} in a small saucepan. Bring to a gentle simmer, whisking, and cook for 2 minutes. Reduce the heat and whisk in {{sbutter}} a little at a time until smooth.','Put the carrots in a saucepan with finely minced {{onion}}, {{parsley}}, {{pepper}}, all the drawn-butter sauce and {{braisewater}}. Cover and simmer very gently for 25–30 minutes, stirring occasionally, until the onion and carrots are completely tender.','Add a small splash of water if the sauce begins to catch. Serve hot.'],'The missing half-cup thin drawn-butter component is supplied as a measured small sauce, adapted from the same-book drawn-butter formulas. Carrot and onion weights and extra braising water are modern choices; the long onion-and-carrot finish remains.')
r['source']+=' Component sources retained: Melted or Drawn Butter Nos. 1 and 2, same book; source IDs historical-e0e4b8deedde8fadfac6e33d and historical-d4cf4501f75ff84e1bd4f983.'
